      <p>Shopping performance is decided in the product feed long before it is decided in the campaign. Most underperforming Shopping accounts do not have a bidding problem. They have titles Google cannot match to a search, attributes that are missing, and a third of the catalogue disapproved without anybody noticing.</p>
      <p>Bids cannot fix a product title. So the feed is treated as part of the campaign work, not as somebody else's job.</p>

      <h2>The feed comes first</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Merchant Center diagnostics.</strong> Disapprovals, warnings and the items silently not serving.</li>
        <li><strong>Product titles rewritten</strong> around how people actually search, rather than how the manufacturer named it. Brand, product type, key attribute, in the order buyers type them.</li>
        <li><strong>Attributes and identifiers completed.</strong> GTINs, brand, condition, size, colour, material. Missing attributes limit which searches you are eligible for at all.</li>
        <li><strong>Custom labels by margin</strong>, so the campaign can bid differently on a product making 60 percent and one making 8 percent.</li>
        <li><strong>Availability and price accuracy</strong>, because stale data causes disapprovals and wasted clicks on things nobody can buy.</li>
        <li><strong>Promotions and sale price feeds</strong> where they apply.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Shopping and Performance Max, kept apart</h2>
      <p>Run together without structure, Performance Max eats Shopping traffic and reports it as its own. You then cut the Shopping campaign that was working, because its numbers look worse.</p>
      <p>We separate them deliberately: brand excluded from Performance Max, listing groups segmented so margin is visible, and reporting that compares new customer acquisition rather than blended totals. There is more on this in <a href="/learn/performance-max-what-it-hides/">what Performance Max hides</a>.</p>

      <h2>Managed to ROAS, with a caveat</h2>
      <p>Return on ad spend is the usual target, and it is the right one as long as everybody remembers it is a revenue measure, not a profit measure.</p>
      <p>A 4x ROAS on a product with a 20 percent margin loses money. A 2x ROAS on a product with a 70 percent margin is excellent. If you can give us margin by product or category, we can bid to profit rather than to revenue, which is where most of the gains in a mature account come from.</p>
      <p>Where margin data is not available we work to a target ROAS agreed from your blended numbers, and say plainly that it is a proxy.</p>

      <h2>What the account usually needs</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Shopping</strong> for the products with clear search demand, structured by priority and margin</li>
        <li><strong>Performance Max</strong> for catalogue breadth, with brand excluded</li>
        <li><strong>Search</strong> on non-brand category terms where Shopping cannot carry the message</li>
        <li><strong>Dynamic remarketing</strong> with existing buyers excluded for the length of your repurchase cycle</li>
        <li><strong>Brand</strong> as a defensive line item, reported separately, tested rather than assumed</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Not all of it at once. A budget spread across five campaign types collects too few conversions each for bidding to learn from.</p>

      <h2>Tracking for e-commerce</h2>
      <p>Revenue tracking goes wrong in ways that flatter the numbers. Purchase events firing twice, order value passed including tax and shipping in one place and excluding them in another, refunds never subtracted, GA4 and Google Ads reporting different totals for the same day.</p>
      <p>Every engagement starts here, because Smart Bidding optimises towards whatever it is told revenue is. If the number is inflated, it will buy you more of the wrong thing, efficiently.</p>

      <h2>Platforms</h2>
      <p>Shopify, WooCommerce, BigCommerce, Magento and custom builds. The platform mostly determines how the feed is generated and how tracking is installed. The campaign work is the same either way, and feed problems are fixable on all of them.</p>

